		<item>
		<title>WWF Raw Results &#8211; 11/18/96 (&#8220;Stone Cold&#8221; Steve Austin vs. Mankind for the first time ever, fallout from Survivor Series)</title>
		<link>https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/398021-wwf-raw-results-november-18-1996-new-haven/</link>
					<comments>https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/398021-wwf-raw-results-november-18-1996-new-haven/#respond</comments>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Mike Tedesco]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:02:24 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Featured Top Story]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Retro]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[1996]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Connecticut]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Monday Night Raw]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Monday Night War]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[New Haven]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[November 18]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[retro recap]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Survivor Series]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWE]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWE RAW]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWE RAW Results]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWE results]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWF]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWF Raw]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWF Raw Results]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWF results]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWF vs. WCW]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.wrestleview.com/?p=398021</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>WWF Monday Night Raw ResultsNovember 18, 1996New Haven, Connecticut (New Haven Coliseum)Commentary: Vince McMahon, Jerry “The King” Lawler,&#8230;</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/398021-wwf-raw-results-november-18-1996-new-haven/">WWF Raw Results &#8211; 11/18/96 (&#8220;Stone Cold&#8221; Steve Austin vs. Mankind for the first time ever, fallout from Survivor Series)</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com">WWE News, WWE Results, AEW News, AEW Results</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<figure class="wp-block-image size-full is-resized"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="750" height="382" src="https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-9.png" alt="" class="wp-image-398023" style="aspect-ratio:1.9634483060875663;width:187px;height:auto" srcset="https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-9.png 750w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-9-600x306.png 600w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-9-379x193.png 379w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-9-550x280.png 550w" sizes="(max-width: 750px) 100vw, 750px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>WWF Monday Night Raw Results<br>November 18, 1996<br>New Haven, Connecticut (New Haven Coliseum)<br>Commentary: Vince McMahon, Jerry “The King” Lawler, and Jim Ross<br>Results by: Mike Tedesco of Wrestleview.com</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The sound of glass shattering is heard, and “Stone Cold” Steve Austin comes out to a nice ovation. Vince McMahon welcomes us to the show. This was supposed to be a Tough Man Competition between Austin and Vader, but it was switched to Mankind after Vader was injured.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Replay:</strong> <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/397714-wwf-survivor-series-results-november-17-1996-new-york-city/">Last night at the Survivor Series</a>, Yokozuna gave Vader a sambo suplex, landing Vader directly onto his shoulder. Jim Ross says Vader will be back in action soon.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As Austin is heading to the ring, Mankind attacks him from behind! They start brawling down the aisleway. Mankind rakes Austin’s eyes and clubs him as he stumbles up the aisle. Austin tackles Mankind on the floor, and they brawl. WWF referees run out to try to separate the two men. Mankind pushes through and takes a referee down to get back to Austin. Mankind is backed up, and Austin charges and hurls himself at Mankind with a clothesline on the floor. They go down by the ringside area, and Mankind takes Austin down. Mankind reverses a whip, sending Austin into the ring steps.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The WWF officials and referees crowd the two men at ringside. Mankind manages to grab Austin and punches him down near the steps. Mankind goes for a running knee, but Austin moves.</p>



<h3 id="stone-cold-steve-austin-vs-mankind-w-paul-bearer" class="wp-block-heading">“Stone Cold” Steve Austin vs. Mankind w/ Paul Bearer</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Earl Hebner</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They finally get in the ring, and the referee officially starts the match. Austin viciously stomps Mankind down in the corner, pushing him out over the ropes. Austin goes to the apron and drops an elbow on Mankind, driving him to the floor. Austin comes off the apron with a sledge to the spine. Mankind quickly pulls Austin out of the ring and attacks him. Mankind bounces him off the ring steps and rakes his eyes. Mankind gets in the ring and pulls Austin in. Mankind stomps him before choking him. The referee backs Mankind up. Austin pulls himself up and catches Mankind with a clothesline before punching away at him. Austin catches Mankind stumbling and punches him. Mankind falls back and through the ropes! Mankind gets to his feet and grabs a chair, but Austin attacks him. Austin grabs the chair, but the referee takes it away. Mankind then punches Austin down. Austin reverses a whip into the barricade, and Mankind flies over it and into the crowd!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Austin pulls Mankind back to the ringside area and gets him in the ring. Austin sends Mankind into the ropes. Mankind ducks a clothesline and applies the Mandible Claw. Austin gets out with a low blow! The referee didn’t see it because he was dealing with Paul Bearer. Both men are down.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, Mankind puts Austin on the top rope. Austin pokes him in the eyes and leaps, but Mankind punches him out of mid-air. Mankind chokes Austin in the corner and squeals before choking him with his boot. Austin is hanging over the apron, so Mankind goes to the apron to drop a leg on him! Austin is dangling over the floor before Mankind pulls him down. Mankind drops Austin throat-first on the barricade and gets in the ring. Mankind sits and rocks in the ring as Austin struggles to get up. Austin grabs Mankind’s hair and punches away at him. Austin gets on the apron and goes to suplex him out of the ring, but Mankind fights it. Mankind then suplexes Austin into the ring.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Austin reverses a whip, but Mankind counters with a swinging neckbreaker. Paul Bearer likes what he’s seeing. Mankind drops a leg on Austin before smashing his face on the canvas. Austin reverses a whip and applies a sleeper hold. Mankind quickly drives him into the corner to break the hold. Mankind charges, but Austin boots him back. Austin charges, but Mankind clotheslines him down. Mankind sets up for a pulling piledriver, but Austin gives him a back body drop to the apron. Mankind then grabs Austin’s head and snaps him off the top rope. Mankind goes to the top rope, but Austin grabs him and punches away at him before slamming him down to the canvas. Austin stomps Mankind before choking him. Austin stomps Mankind and punches him out of the ring. Austin goes to the apron and stomps his head. Mankind manages to pull Austin to the floor and bounces him off the steel steps. Mankind chokes Austin, but Austin gets out of it and drops him throat-first on the barricade.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Austin gets Mankind into the ring and hits a snapmare. Austin goes to the second rope and hits a Bombs Away elbow for a near fall. Austin puts Mankind in the corner and shoulders him in the midsection several times. Mankind starts to fall through the ropes and is holding on. Austin grabs Mankind and punches away at him before he falls to the floor.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>The Executioner</strong> runs down to the ring, led by Paul Bearer, and he attacks Austin from behind. Mankind and The Executioner double-team Austin as the referee calls for the bell.</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-disqualification-stone-cold-steve-austin" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Disqualification: “Stone Cold” Steve Austin</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>The Undertaker</strong> runs down to even the odds. Undertaker punches Mankind over the top rope before punching The Executioner down. Executioner rolls out of the ring. Undertaker turns, and Austin clotheslines him over the top rope. Undertaker lands on his feet and angrily stares at Austin. The Undertaker gets into the ring, but Austin bails out and retreats up the aisleway. Austin stares at The Undertaker as he backs up.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974780 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> Absolutely phenomenal match with Mankind and “Stone Cold” Steve Austin. Without a doubt, it’s one of the best television matches I’ve covered in this series so far. I remember this being an extra feature on Mick Foley’s <em>Hard Knocks and Cheap Pops</em> DVD in 2001 and watching the hell out of it, along with the Shawn Michaels match from Mind Games. Matches like this helped solidify my lifelong love of pro wrestling. I knew it was coming and couldn’t wait to start recapping this show.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974782 wp-block-paragraph">This is the first time Mankind and Steve Austin ever fought in a singles match, and they had a wild, realistic brawl where both men felt evenly matched and had something to prove. In <em>Have a Nice Day!</em>, Foley mentioned that he and Austin had been discussing the contracts they had been offered and that neither man was at the level of Marc Mero’s deal. Foley said that frustration motivated them, and watching the match, you can absolutely see it. Once again, this is a different style of wrestling than we’ve been seeing in the WWF. This style will soon become more of the norm, but at the time, it was pretty groundbreaking. It was athletic, violent, and totally in-your-face, a far cry from the slower main-event style WCW often featured, even though WCW’s mid-card and cruiserweight divisions were doing tremendous work.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974784 wp-block-paragraph">As seems to be the case with many great Mankind matches early on, the finish left something to be desired. Austin and Mankind were in the middle of this knockdown, drag-out brawl when The Executioner, in the laziest of finishes, simply ran in and attacked Austin. Still, like the match with Shawn Michaels, this match was so good that you can’t let the last ten seconds tarnish what they accomplished in the first twelve minutes. In another one of those situations where an injury turns into something advantageous, Mankind taking Vader’s spot led to something truly memorable. I don’t think Austin and Vader could have done something this good.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974785 wp-block-paragraph">All this being said, Foley mentions that he had a contract conversation with Vince McMahon after these tapings and expressed his frustration over Mero’s contract and not making much more money than he had as an independent wrestler the year before. The payoffs for Foley would become higher, but without a signed contract, the WWF wasn’t going to put its full promotional machine behind the Mankind character until that changed. As such, we’ll actually be seeing Mankind featured much less following this set of television tapings. He’ll still be around, but the major promo time and important storylines that helped make him feel so fresh in 1996 are about to dry up for a while.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> The Survivor Series encore is coming tomorrow night at 8 p.m. Eastern, only on pay-per-view.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Replay:</strong> Moments ago, as The Undertaker finished dealing with Mankind and The Executioner, “Stone Cold” Steve Austin clotheslined him over the top rope. Undertaker landed on his feet and scared Austin away.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Ahmed Johnson</strong> enters through the crowd and takes a seat to watch the next match.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“I know you want me.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Sunny</strong> makes her way to the ring to the tune of her new theme song. She introduces a dear friend of hers, Faarooq, the leader of the Nation of Domination.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Faarooq heads to the ring carrying a 2&#215;4 with PG-13 and Clarence Mason leading the way. PG-13, consisting of J.C. Ice and Wolfie D, rap the lyrics to the Nation of Domination theme. Faarooq gets in the ring and does the Nation Salute. PG-13 kneels at the ringside and gives him back the salute.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Acclaim Slam of the Week:</strong> Rocky Maivia was the Sole Survivor in his debut match at <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/397714-wwf-survivor-series-results-november-17-1996-new-york-city/">Survivor Series last night</a>, eliminating Crush and Goldust to win.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ahmed Johnson is seen sitting in the crowd. Sunny joins the commentary team.</p>



<h3 id="faarooq-w-the-nation-of-domination-pg-13-j-c-ice-and-wolfie-d-and-clarence-mason-vs-savio-vega" class="wp-block-heading">Faarooq w/ The Nation of Domination (PG-13 (J.C. Ice and Wolfie D) and Clarence Mason) vs. Savio Vega</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Jack Doan</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Faarooq starts the match by punching away at Savio Vega. Faarooq knocks him into the corner and whips him to the opposite corner. Vega sidesteps an avalanche and hits an arm drag, followed by a dropkick. Vega knocks Faarooq into the corner and does the ten punches. Vega sends Faarooq to the opposite corner and hits a monkey flip. Vega wrenches Faarooq’s arm before going to a hammerlock. Faarooq does a standing switch, but Vega turns it back on him. Faarooq elbows him in the face and stuns him with some punches. Faarooq sends Vega into the ropes, but Vega ducks a clothesline and hits a running crossbody block for a two-count. Vega hits a hip toss for another two-count. Vega goes back to the arm wrench. Faarooq gets out by raking the eyes and punching him. Vega reverses a whip and goes for a leapfrog, but Faarooq SMASHES him with a spinebuster for a two-count.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Faarooq starts attacking the kidney area of Vega to send a message to Ahmed Johnson, who is in the crowd. Faarooq whips him hard into the corner and gives Johnson the Nation Salute. Faarooq connects with a snap suplex and picks up a two-count. Vega chops Faarooq back and bounces him off the top turnbuckle. Faarooq quickly bounces him on the top turnbuckle and folds him up like an accordion with a back suplex.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, Faarooq is sitting on Vega’s back applying a rear chin lock. Faarooq releases the hold and jumps on Vega’s back. Faarooq does it again. When he tries a third time, Vega rolls over and knees him in the groin! Vega slowly gets up and goes for a splash, but Faarooq gets the knees up.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Both men take their time before Faarooq gets up and attacks Vega. Faarooq does another Nation Salute and goes for a slam, but Vega counters with an inside cradle for a near fall. Faarooq kicks Vega and sends him into the ropes, but Vega hits a facebuster. Vega catches his breath in the corner. Vega mounts Faarooq and punches him in the head several times. Vega hits a scoop slam before attempting an elbow drop, but Faarooq moves. Faarooq stomps Vega and hits a sloppy scoop slam. Faarooq heads to the top rope, but Vega crotches him up there. Vega chops Faarooq’s chest and climbs to the top rope for a superplex. Faarooq shifts, and they fall off the top rope to the floor in a heap! That could have been awful. That clearly wasn’t planned.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Faarooq quickly gets up and throws Vega into the ring. Faarooq sends Vega into the ropes, but he lowers his head and eats a kick. Vega sends Faarooq into the ropes and hits a spinning heel kick. Vega hits the ropes, but he flies over the top rope when Faarooq ducks. Wolfie D, who hasn’t been identified by name, grabs Vega, but Vega attacks him. J.C. Ice hits Vega from behind with the 2&#215;4! They roll Vega into the ring, and Faarooq picks up the pin.</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-pinfall-faarooq" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Pinfall: Faarooq</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ahmed Johnson is furious and charges to the ring. Johnson grabs Ice and slams him HARD onto the floor. Johnson grabs the 2&#215;4 and chases the Nation of Domination out of the ring! The crowd roars as Johnson stands in the ring for the first time since August. Faarooq, who is in the crowd, shouts that this isn’t over yet.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Johnson grabs a microphone and says he lay in a bed for five months after Faarooq jumped him from behind. Faarooq had better never put his hands on him. It’s payback time. Faarooq is going down. Johnson gets the crowd chanting, “You’re going down!” Johnson then heads into the crowd to go after Faarooq.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974827 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> Super boring match with Faarooq and Savio Vega, but it did have a hot finish with Ahmed Johnson charging the ring for the first time in months. Johnson’s promo skills haven’t improved much since he was gone, but the intensity that got him over was still there. A lot has changed in the three months he was off television, though. Will Johnson be able to pick up where he left off?</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974829 wp-block-paragraph">Also, that huge botch where Faarooq and Vega fell to the floor on the superplex spot was <strong>WILD</strong>.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974830 wp-block-paragraph">It is interesting to see how suddenly the Nation of Domination came into being. Faarooq debuted the new look and theme at Survivor Series, and now PG-13 is with him, even though they still haven’t been identified by name. If you didn’t remember that they had two random matches on Raw last year, you have no clue who these guys are. It’s not much of a “nation” yet, but it will be interesting to watch him build it as it goes. Either way, this is already light years better than him parading around in that ridiculous gladiator uniform.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Milton Bradley Karate Fighters Holiday Tournament:</strong> Jerry “The King” Lawler and Todd Pettengill welcome us back to the tournament where Mr. Bob Backlund and Sunny will face off. Lawler says he likes the way Sunny twists the knobs. Before the match begins, Backlund says Sunny has been living a slovenly lifestyle. Backlund says Headstone will give her some morality. Sunny beats Backlund in a match. Backlund flips out as Sunny taunts him. Backlund tells her that her preponderance is an abomination to him. Sunny will face Sable in the Semifinals.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974833 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> I can’t believe I have thoughts about the Milton Bradley Karate Fighters Holiday Tournament, but on their brackets, they have Mr. Perfect vs. WWF Champion Sycho Sid in the Semifinals. I get that these are all pre-taped, but considering the story I’ll write about in the Wrap-Up, how are they going to feature Mr. Perfect on this broadcast any further?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Monday Night Raw is brought to you by Footaction, Snickers, and Starburst Fruit Twists.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Locker Room Segment</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Jim Ross is in the locker room with WWF Champion Sycho Sid, who is lifting weights. Ross will have an interview with the new champion in a little while.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back at commentary, Vince McMahon talks about what happened last night at Survivor Series. Sid had a problem with a cameraman and pulled the camera off his shoulder. Sid then went on to hit José Lothario in the chest with the camera. Lothario clutched his chest, which distracted Shawn Michaels. Sid attacked Michaels and threw him into the ring, but the referee was knocked out. Sid hit Michaels with the camera and put him in the ring for a Powerbomb to win.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">McMahon says Michaels accompanied Lothario back to San Antonio, Texas. Lothario refused medical attention. McMahon hopes Lothario is resting comfortably at home. McMahon wants to reflect back on what kind of champion Shawn Michaels was, not that he won’t be again. According to McMahon, Michaels was the most charismatic and flamboyant WWF Champion they’ve had. Michaels is the greatest showman in WWF history, but many people don’t like him. Michaels dances, has long hair, and is a ladies’ man. Jerry Lawler says Michaels is cocky and arrogant, which McMahon gives him.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">McMahon says last night, Shawn Michaels proved something to everyone. When Michaels had the opportunity to win after hitting Sweet Chin Music, Michaels went outside to aid his mentor, José Lothario. In essence, he cared more about his fellow man than the WWF Championship. That speaks volumes about Shawn Michaels. McMahon says, “Yes, he may be a ladies’ man, but considering what he did last night, Shawn Michaels proved beyond a shadow of a doubt, as far as I’m concerned, he’s a man’s man and deserving of a rematch with Sid.”</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974847 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> This was a bit of damage control being done by Vince McMahon. His top babyface had just been booed out of Madison Square Garden the night before, which could set off a snowball effect of being booed in other places, so he wanted to take a moment to rebrand himself a bit. McMahon acknowledged that there are aspects of the character people can dislike, but pointed out the bigger aspects of the storyline, including José Lothario’s medical emergency the night before, saying that Michaels cared more about his fellow man than any title. That makes him a man’s man in McMahon’s eyes.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974849 wp-block-paragraph">I think McMahon gets a lot of flak for his commentary, and deservingly so, for not being completely familiar with the moves, frequently relying on tropes like “What a maneuver” and “FUHGETABOUT,” but he was super effective with segments like these. As the guy who, by and large, writes the stories, he’s super passionate about people seeing them in the way he wants. He could do a really nice job of painting a picture and explaining a story that maybe didn’t land in execution with a segment. When a guy was in trouble, he could also find a silver lining and build them up. After all, he’s not just the guy writing the stories. He’s invested time and money in each character on the screen and has a strong interest in seeing them succeed. This was an example of that, and he did an admirable job of trying to turn the narrative around and get guys to like Shawn Michaels, too. Will it work? Only time will tell.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Leif Cassidy and Bob “Spark Plugg” Holly head to the ring with <strong>Captain Lou Albano</strong> randomly hovering behind them. Albano goes all the way to the ringside area and is told he’s with the wrong team.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Philip LaFon and Doug Furnas head to the ring, and Albano joins them. Albano then joins the Spanish commentary team.</p>



<h3 id="tag-team-matchleif-cassidy-and-bob-spark-plugg-holly-vs-philip-lafon-and-doug-furnas" class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Tag Team Match</strong><br>Leif Cassidy and Bob “Spark Plugg” Holly vs. Philip LaFon and Doug Furnas</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Mike Chioda</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Philip LaFon will start against Leif Cassidy. They lock up and tussle to the corner. LaFon gives a clean break. Cassidy isn’t happy. They lock hands and go for a test of strength. Cassidy knees LaFon and applies an overhead wristlock before sending him into the ropes. LaFon shoulder tackles Cassidy and hits the ropes. LaFon kicks Cassidy and blocks a roll-up. LaFon then takes him down with an armbreaker. Cassidy grapevines the head, so LaFon jackknife pins him. Cassidy bridges up, and they start trading strikes. LaFon then kicks Cassidy out of the ring. We hear Captain Lou Albano pretending to speak Spanish with those commentators. Bob Holly tags in, as does Doug Furnas.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Interview:</strong> WWF Tag Team Champions Owen Hart and The British Bulldog are backstage with Clarence Mason. Vince McMahon asks if they were impressed with Furnas and LaFon. Bulldog says they let their guards down, so that’s the last victory Furnas and LaFon will get. Hart says he’s pissed. Hart says they got lucky. A worthless Frenchman and Okie won’t beat them again. Mason says you can’t waltz into the WWF and get a title shot. They’ve got to climb the ladder.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back to the action, Cassidy takes LaFon down and tags Holly in.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, LaFon attacks Cassidy and Holly. Cassidy rakes his eyes. Holly sends LaFon into the ropes and hits a dropkick for a two-count. Holly puts LaFon in the corner and stomps him. Holly goes for a cheap shot on Furnas, and Furnas knocks him back. Holly attacks LaFon and hits a scoop slam. Holly goes to the second rope, but LaFon avoids a diving elbow drop.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Furnas tags in and runs over Holly before knocking Cassidy off the apron. Furnas sends Holly into the ropes for a dropkick to pick up a two-count. Furnas sends Holly back into the ropes for a hurricanrana. Cassidy quickly breaks up the pin. Cassidy tags in, and Furnas clotheslines him down. LaFon tags in, and Cassidy hits him with a dragon suplex for a near fall! Cassidy signals for the end as he slaps LaFon in the face. LaFon gets out of a full nelson and hits a cobra clutch suplex, spiking Cassidy on his head, for the win. Furnas tackled Holly for good measure.</p>



<h3 id="winners-by-pinfall-philip-lafon-and-doug-furnas" class="wp-block-heading">Winners by Pinfall: Philip LaFon and Doug Furnas</h3>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974870 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> There was nothing wrong with the wrestling in this, but the crowd was completely dead. Philip LaFon and Doug Furnas tried to drag the people into it, but no one knows who they are. They showed up the night before at Survivor Series and pinned The British Bulldog and Owen Hart, but that’s not enough to get people to care. The Stalker got some video packages. Who are LaFon and Furnas? Why should anyone care? A simple video package, or even a promo before the match, could have helped generate a bit of a reaction.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974871 wp-block-paragraph">Leif Cassidy is teaming with Bob Holly here because Marty Jannetty hurt his foot badly at Survivor Series. With the severe lack of tag teams (The Godwinns are really the last team standing aside from the champions, but they’re faces), they had to throw in a random substitute for Jannetty, which wound up being Bob Holly. Probably the most interesting thing in the whole match was Captain Lou Albano getting confused about which team he was supposed to come down with, and his performance at the Spanish commentary table.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Segment</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WWF Champion Sycho Sid is talking with Jim Ross backstage. Sid takes Ross’ handkerchief and wipes his belt with it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> The Survivor Series encore airs tomorrow night on pay-per-view at 8 p.m. Eastern.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WWF Champion Sycho Sid heads to the ring looking as intense as ever. We’ll hear from him next.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> You can buy a piece of the top rope from Survivor Series for $59.00.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Next month, Milton Bradley Karate Fighters presents the WWF In Your House show.</p>



<h3 id="wwf-champion-sycho-sid-addresses-his-victory" class="wp-block-heading">WWF Champion Sycho Sid addresses his victory</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Jim Ross is in the ring with WWF Champion Sycho Sid. Ross asks the crowd what they think of him, and they cheer. Ross congratulates him on his victory, though it was somewhat controversial. Sid had thousands of fans in Madison Square Garden cheering him on. Many people said he was too unstable, but he’s now the man. Does he have any remorse for his actions last night regarding Shawn Michaels or the 62-year-old José Lothario?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sid says no because Lothario, a man he has great respect for, put his “ass” on the apron and became part of the game. When you play the game with the man, then only misery can come with that. Ross asks about a rematch for the Heartbreak Kid. Sid says if Michaels wants a rematch, then he’s got it. Sid beat him once and will do it again because he is the man, the master, and the ruler of the world.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ross says the superstars lining up to challenge him include Vader, “Stone Cold” Steve Austin, and The Undertaker. On December 15, he’ll face the legendary Bret “Hitman” Hart for the WWF Championship. Will Sid be a fighting champion? Sid says the people made him a champion. For the people, he’ll be a fighting champion. Ross asks about Hart specifically. Sid says if Hart is trying to make opportunities by going through his door, well, his comeback to the WWF will end on December 15 because he has to face him. Hart has to face him… the master. The game is simple. Two men enter. One person survives. When the smoke clears, he will be standing because he is the master and the ruler of the world. Sid poses in the ring, and fireworks go off overhead as the show comes to an end.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974892 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> That wasn’t a great promo from Sycho Sid. Sid stumbled over his words a lot and didn’t say much of interest. The only thing I liked was him talking about José Lothario and saying Lothario put himself in the game and had to pay the consequences. The crowd was definitely on his side, but repeating his catchphrase twice in one three-minute promo made this feel more like Sid doing Sid than the start of a red-hot title reign.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974894 wp-block-paragraph">That said, there was no appearance by Shawn Michaels tonight, even though they kept teasing it throughout the commentary. The angle to close Survivor Series wasn’t any less tacky with José Lothario having a supposed heart attack. For them not to have a real follow-up other than saying Lothario refused medical attention is really lame, letting people go another week wondering if this guy is going to live or not. I pretty much hate any heart attack or death angle in pro wrestling, and Lothario’s deal is no different.</p>



<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>



<h3 id="quick-match-results" class="wp-block-heading">Quick Match Results</h3>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974899 wp-block-paragraph">&#8212; “Stone Cold” Steve Austin def. Mankind via DQ<br>&#8212; Faarooq def. Savio Vega<br>&#8212; Philip LaFon* and Doug Furnas def. Leif Cassidy* and Bob “Spark Plugg” Holly in a Tag Team Match</p>



<h3 id="mikes-wrap-up-what-we-learned-from-raw" class="wp-block-heading">Mike’s Wrap-Up: What We Learned from Raw</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This was a solid post-Survivor Series episode of Raw, but the show peaked immediately with the fantastic “Stone Cold” Steve Austin vs. Mankind match. That match felt like another glimpse of where the WWF is heading: more physical, more chaotic, more realistic, and much less polished than the New Generation style that has dominated the company for the last few years.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The WWF Championship scene is also in reset mode. Sycho Sid is now the champion, and the crowd is clearly behind him, but his first promo as champion was not exactly inspiring. At the same time, Vince McMahon spent a good portion of the show trying to reframe Shawn Michaels after the Madison Square Garden crowd rejected him at Survivor Series. Michaels is still positioned as the heroic former champion, but it is becoming harder to ignore that not every audience is buying him that way.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Nation of Domination also got its first real Raw spotlight, and Ahmed Johnson returned to confront Faarooq. The match with Savio Vega was boring, but the presentation is already a major improvement over the gladiator costume. Faarooq finally feels like he has a direction.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There was also some major business drama hanging over the show with Curt Hennig. According to the Wrestling Observer, Hennig had agreed to terms with Eric Bischoff and no-showed several scheduled WWF appearances, including Live Wire, Superstars, house show bookings, a personal appearance, and Survivor Series. The WWF responded with legal threats, claiming WCW was interfering with a valid WWF contract, while WCW reportedly maintained that Hennig would not debut until after his WWF deal expired. At one point during the week, the WWF believed things had been smoothed over and that Hennig might even return under a new big-money wrestling contract, but after he missed the pay-per-view, the company apparently realized he was all but gone. With Hennig recently involved in the Hunter Hearst Helmsley/Marc Mero story, this is another example of the WWF trying to build angles while the Monday Night War keeps pulling talent and plans in different directions.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WCW still won the night, making it twenty-one straight weeks for Nitro. Raw had some strong matches, especially Austin vs. Mankind, but this felt more like a company resetting after a major pay-per-view than a show that would close the ratings gap overnight.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Also, as a side note, two weeks ago, the WWF abruptly moved Raw from 8:57 p.m. Eastern to 7:57 p.m. Eastern to get a three-minute jump on WCW Monday Nitro rather than starting halfway through Nitro’s broadcast. In a wonderfully petty bit of ratings gamesmanship, WCW responded less than two weeks later by starting Nitro at 7:55 p.m. instead of 8:00, offsetting whatever head start WWF was trying to gain. The pettiness of the Monday Night War is really something to behold, but when you think about what it probably takes to adjust a television schedule just to win a few extra minutes, it’s pretty amazing that a company would be willing to go that far. Every minute matters.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974912 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>WWF Raw (USA Network, live): 2.41 (Record: 16-37)</strong><br><strong>WCW Nitro (TNT, live): 3.16 (Record: 37-16)</strong></p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795202974913 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Winner: WCW Nitro (21-week streak)</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Email – <a href="mailto:mike@wrestleview.com">mike@wrestleview.com</a><br>X – <a href="https://x.com/MikeTedescoWV">@MikeTedescoWV</a></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/397714-wwf-survivor-series-results-november-17-1996-new-york-city/">Survivor Series 1996</a> | <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/397331-wwf-raw-results-november-11-1996-fort-wayne/">Last week’s Raw</a> | Last week’s Nitro</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Thanks for reading!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>Part of Wrestleview’s Monday Night War: 30 Years Later series, with weekly Raw and Nitro recaps every Thursday.</em></p>
